@group(:concurrency)
mod Std.Otp.Registry
  ## `Pid` vs `GenServer` SEPARATION and `whereis` PARTIALITY — the "the type must not
  ## lie" conformance obligations (build-out map G7; typed-BEAM defects F-2 and F-2c).
  ##
  ## Two ways an OTP surface type can silently lie, both fixed here:
  ##
  ## **F-2 — a raw `Pid` is not a `GenServer`.** A bare process id carries no protocol:
  ## you cannot type a `call`/`cast` against it (there is no request/reply type). Yet a
  ## surface that makes `Pid` and `GenServer` the same type lets you send a typed request
  ## to an arbitrary process. Here a `GenServer(q, r)` WRAPS a pid and carries its
  ## protocol; a bare `Pid` cannot be used where a `GenServer(q, r)` is expected
  ## (`server_pid(MkPid)` fails to unify). Turning a pid INTO a typed server is an
  ## explicit assertion, `expect_server` — the FFI trust boundary, visible in the term,
  ## not a silent identity.
  ##
  ## **F-2c — `whereis(name)` may find nothing.** A registered name can be unregistered
  ## or its process dead, so `whereis` returns a `PidOption`, never a bare `Pid` it cannot
  ## guarantee. Consuming the result (`with_pid`) must handle the absent case: a consumer
  ## that ignores `NoPid` is non-exhaustive and REJECTED by totality certification (see
  ## `otp_registry_test.exs`). So "the name is surely registered" is not expressible in a
  ## total program.
  ##
  ## **Honest scope.** `Pid` is abstract (identity/liveness not modelled); the protocol
  ## types `q`/`r` are phantom on the handle (the reduction/typing of the actual
  ## request-reply is `Std.Otp.Proof`/`Std.Otp.SendEffect`). This module is the surface
  ## honesty discipline — no `Pid`/`GenServer` conflation, no total `whereis`.

  ## An abstract process id.
  type Pid = MkPid

  ## A typed server handle: a pid PLUS its request/reply protocol. NOT a `Pid`.
  type GenServer(q: Type, r: Type) indices ()
    MkGen : Pid -> GenServer(q, r)

  ## The result of a name lookup: a pid, or nothing (unregistered / dead).
  type PidOption = NoPid | SomePid(Pid)

  ## The pid a `GenServer` wraps is recoverable (a server IS a pid + protocol).
  fn server_pid({q: Type}, {r: Type}, g: GenServer(q, r)) -> Pid = match g
    MkGen(p) -> p

  ## Turning a raw pid into a TYPED server — the explicit protocol assertion / FFI trust
  ## boundary. This is the ONLY way to get a `GenServer` from a bare `Pid`; it is a
  ## visible claim, not a silent coercion.
  fn expect_server({q: Type}, {r: Type}, p: Pid) -> GenServer(q, r) = MkGen(p)

  ## **TOTALITY.** Consuming a `whereis` result must handle the absent case — a consumer
  ## omitting `NoPid` fails exhaustiveness certification. `with_pid` is the total
  ## eliminator: given a found-continuation and an absent-continuation, it always
  ## produces a result.
  fn with_pid({s: Type}, o: PidOption, on_found: (Pid) -> s, on_absent: () -> s) -> s = match o
    SomePid(p) -> on_found(p)
    NoPid()    -> on_absent()

  ## Non-vacuity: a lookup can find a pid or find nothing.
  fn found() -> PidOption = SomePid(MkPid)
  fn absent() -> PidOption = NoPid
end
